v9k/tools/ -- standing instruments for the Victor 9000 port.  Host-side
Python 3, no dependencies, not built by victorow.mak.

These are NOT probes.  A probe answers one question once; these are read
every time a change is measured, and one of them is a build rule.  They
lived in ".probe/" until 2026-08-09, where the name said the opposite of
what CLAUDE.md's hard rule 4 required of them.


mzsize.py -- what an MZ image needs at LOAD, and the smallest Victor that
can run it.  CLAUDE.md hard rule 4: "Before raising SBSIZ/RBSIZ/MAXSP/
MAXRP, measure the image, not DGROUP."  Run this, not "ls -l" -- the file
size is not the requirement, because DOS also adds minalloc paragraphs for
.bss and the stack.

    python3 v9k/tools/mzsize.py ckermitw.exe
    python3 v9k/tools/mzsize.py -a 393216 ckermitw.exe   # a 384K machine
    python3 v9k/tools/mzsize.py -a 0 ckermitw.exe        # requirement alone

Quote the REQUIREMENT, not the spare: a Victor takes RAM in 128K
increments from 128K to 896K, so "spare" is a fact about the bench machine
and not about the port.


pktstat.py -- counts a C-Kermit packet log in BOTH directions: packets and
types, WIRE BYTES, longest packet, timeouts, and retransmissions by either
end.  PORTING.md SS16l is where the two one-line greps it replaces are
recorded.

    python3 v9k/tools/pktstat.py s16afAG.pkt
    python3 v9k/tools/pktstat.py --rxbytes 44720 --rxfull 741 s16afAJ.pkt

It reads SEND legs correctly as of 2026-08-09.  It did not before: it
measured only the lines the log-writer sent, so on a Victor-send leg it
reported the host's ACK stream and counted the HOST's retransmissions --
"longest 49, retransmissions 0" for vic_tran2.pkt, whose longest packet is
3,716 and which contains four VICTOR resends.  A remote retransmission has
no marker of its own; it is the same sequence number arriving twice
running, which is what the tool now looks for.

Wire bytes are the reason to reach for it.  The bench does not repeat to
better than ~1.3 s (NEXT_SESSION item 5b), so an effect worth less than
that cannot be timed -- but if it shows up as traffic it can be COUNTED,
exactly and deterministically.  That is how NEXT_SESSION item 5a is meant
to be run.

Two things it does that are not obvious and that a hand count gets wrong.
The end-of-line terminator is a byte on the wire but appears only in SENT
lines, so it is added back per packet, from the S packet's negotiated EOL
rather than from an assumption -- without that the two directions are not
comparable.  And the total is computed twice, from the line length and
from the packet's own LEN field, so a truncated log or a raw LF inside a
packet is reported instead of silently shortening the answer.

--rxbytes reconciles the log against the Victor's ISR counter, which counts
the same bytes by a wholly independent route:

    host wire bytes - rxbytes = rxfull + startup offset

rxbytes counts only bytes the ISR STORED, so an overrun shows up exactly:
16af leg AJ sent 45,461, counted 44,720, and the 741 difference is that
leg's published rxfull to the byte.  A clean leg leaves a small startup
constant (-11 with no startup timeout, +28 when a timeout means the Victor
missed the first S packet).  Any other residual means the leg is not
usable for a byte comparison, and the tool says so.

Note that "wire bytes" in PORTING.md SS16u onward is the Victor's rxbytes,
which is that startup constant away from what the host actually sent.  This
tool reports the host's figure; quote which one you mean.


mapoffset.py -- turns a byte offset from the v9k: counters (peakat,
stallat, lostat, lostend) into "which packet, and was it a resend", by
adding up wire lengths from the host's packet log.  This is the tool that
settled the rxpeak question in PORTING.md SS16m.

    python3 v9k/tools/mapoffset.py s16afAG.pkt --rxbytes 37568 15673


A caution that applies to all three: they read the HOST's packet log and
the VICTOR's counters, which do not measure the same interval (SS16u).
Quote the pair, never one alone.
